---
source: crates/pogo-cli/tests/cli.rs
expression: help
---
Write data to the target, optionally waiting for a response

Usage: pogo serial send [OPTIONS] <DATA>

Arguments:
  <DATA>
          Data to send, or "-" to read stdin

Options:
      --line-ending <LINE_ENDING>
          Line ending appended to the data
          
          [default: lf]
          [possible values: lf, crlf, none]

      --hex
          Interpret data as hex bytes, e.g. "de ad be ef"

      --expect <REGEX>
          Capture until a response line matches

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

  -V, --version
          Print version

Global options:
  -d, --device <SELECTOR>
          Target device: alias, USB serial, port, or "auto"

      --probe <SELECTOR>
          Probe device [default: "probe" alias, else auto]

  -f, --format <FORMAT>
          Output format [default: human]
          
          [env: POGO_FORMAT=]
          [possible values: human, json, jsonl]

      --config <PATH>
          Config file
          
          [env: POGO_CONFIG=]

      --timeout <DURATION>
          Overall deadline for this command, e.g. 500ms, 10s, 2m

      --wait-lock <DURATION>
          Wait for a busy device instead of failing

      --dry-run
          Describe what a mutating command would do; change nothing

  -v, --verbose...
          More logs on stderr (repeatable)

  -q, --quiet
          Errors only on stderr

      --no-color
          Disable colors

Writes data with a line ending. With --expect, captures until a response matches,
with the same fault detection as serial capture.

Examples:
  pogo serial send ping --expect pong -f json
  pogo serial send --hex 'de ad be ef' --line-ending none
  echo reboot | pogo serial send -

Exit codes: 0 ok, 4 timeout waiting for --expect, 6 device I/O, 7 target fault, 9 device busy

JSON data: { device, bytes_written, capture }
